{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "04-normalization.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "What types rule out",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> See $\\Omega$ and $Y$ fail to type, see why every typed term reaches a normal form, and what that guarantee costs.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 1 opened with $\\Omega$ looping. $Y$, which gives the untyped calculus its recursion, unfolds for as long as you let it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal 4: Y f",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Both are built on self-application. What will inference say about each?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: omega",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: Y",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The occurs check both times: inside $Y$, $\\lambda x.\\, f\\ (x\\ x)$ has the same $x\\ x$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Every typed term stops",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Is that luck, or can no typed term loop? Watch what a β-step does to types. A redex $(\\lambda x{:}A.\\, M)\\ N$ uses up a function of type $A \\to B$. A redex the step creates is headed by a function of type $A$ or $B$: smaller than $A \\to B$. New work appears only at smaller types, and types cannot shrink for ever. That is not yet a proof, since a step can also copy redexes already inside $N$; Tait's method turns the idea into one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Strong normalization\n> In the simply typed λ-calculus every reduction sequence of a typed term is finite: every typed term has a normal form, and every strategy reaches it (Tait, 1967).",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So a typed term never loops. It can still take a long time: the theorem says reduction ends, not that it ends soon, and a short typed term can have an enormous normal form.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The price",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Every typed term stops, so no fixed-point combinator has a type, and the simply typed calculus has no unbounded recursion: it is not Turing complete. Typed languages add recursion back as a primitive (`fix`, `let rec`), and give up termination with it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Types also refuse some terms that would run fine. Give the identity to a function that uses its argument on an $a$ and on a $b$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal: (λf. pair (f a) (f b)) (λx. x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: a : A, b : B ⊢ (λf. pair (f a) (f b)) (λx. x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "It reduces to the pair of $a$ and $b$, but a simply typed $f$ has one type, and the identity is needed at $A \\to A$ and at $B \\to B$. An identity written once for every type is lesson 7's polymorphism.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Typed numerals",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Much of Church's arithmetic survives. A numeral has type $(\\alpha \\to \\alpha) \\to \\alpha \\to \\alpha$, and multiplication is typed:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: 3",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: mul",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`mul`'s principal type is more general than numerals need: put $A \\to A$ for each of $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$ and it becomes $N \\to N \\to N$, where $N = (A \\to A) \\to A \\to A$ is the type of numerals at $A$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Not all of it survives. Church's $\\mathsf{and} = \\lambda p.\\, \\lambda q.\\, p\\ q\\ p$ passes $p$ to itself:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: and",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> When $p$ is false, $p\\ q\\ p$ returns $p$, which is false. So what could stand in place of the last $p$? Predict whether the result has a type.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λp. λq. p q false",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The same truth table, no self-application, and a type.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A small typed language: numbers, booleans, addition, a test for zero and `if`. Its evaluator is structurally recursive, so Lean accepts it as total: it always stops. With no functions in the language, termination is that easy; for λ-terms it is the theorem above. A program can still be stuck: `1 + true` has no value, and the type checker refuses it before it runs.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "inductive Expr where\n  | num : Nat → Expr\n  | tt : Expr\n  | ff : Expr\n  | add : Expr → Expr → Expr\n  | isZero : Expr → Expr\n  | ite : Expr → Expr → Expr → Expr\n\ninductive T where\n  | nat | bool\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\ndef typeOfE : Expr → Option T\n  | .num _ => some .nat\n  | .tt | .ff => some .bool\n  | .add a b => match typeOfE a, typeOfE b with\n    | some .nat, some .nat => some .nat\n    | _, _ => none\n  | .isZero a => match typeOfE a with\n    | some .nat => some .bool\n    | _ => none\n  | .ite c t e => match typeOfE c, typeOfE t, typeOfE e with\n    | some .bool, some A, some B => if A = B then some A else none\n    | _, _, _ => none\n\ninductive Val where\n  | num : Nat → Val\n  | bool : Bool → Val\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\n/-- Evaluation; none is a program stuck on a type error. -/\ndef evalE : Expr → Option Val\n  | .num n => some (.num n)\n  | .tt => some (.bool true)\n  | .ff => some (.bool false)\n  | .add a b => match evalE a, evalE b with\n    | some (.num m), some (.num n) => some (.num (m + n))\n    | _, _ => none\n  | .isZero a => match evalE a with\n    | some (.num n) => some (.bool (n == 0))\n    | _ => none\n  | .ite c t e => match evalE c with\n    | some (.bool true) => evalE t\n    | some (.bool false) => evalE e\n    | _ => none\n\n#eval evalE (.ite (.isZero (.num 0)) (.num 1) (.num 2))\n#eval evalE (.add (.num 1) .tt)",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem rejects : typeOfE (.add (.num 1) .tt) = none := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "The type checker refuses `1 + true`, the program that gets stuck.",
      "leanSolution": "  rfl",
      "hints": [
        "Both sides compute: `rfl`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λx. λy. x y y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Infer the type of $\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x\\ y\\ y$.",
      "hints": [
        "$x$ takes $y$ twice."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λf. f (λx. x)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Infer the type of $\\lambda f.\\, f\\ (\\lambda x.\\, x)$.",
      "hints": [
        "$f$ is given the identity, of type $\\alpha \\to \\alpha$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λp. λq. p true q",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Church's $\\mathsf{or} = \\lambda p.\\, \\lambda q.\\, p\\ p\\ q$ passes $p$ to itself too, and has no simple type. When $p$ is true, the $p$ it passes is true. Repair it the same way as `and`, and infer the type of the result.",
      "hints": [
        "Replace the middle $p$ by $\\mathsf{true}$: $\\lambda p.\\, \\lambda q.\\, p\\ \\mathsf{true}\\ q$.",
        "$\\mathsf{true} : \\alpha \\to \\beta \\to \\alpha$, and $p$ takes it and then $q$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Self-application has no simple type, so neither $\\Omega$ nor $Y$ does. Every typed term reaches a normal form (strong normalization): a β-step makes new work only at smaller types. The price: no unbounded recursion, and some harmless terms refused, since a simply typed function uses its argument at one type.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Look again at the arrow rules. From $B$, assuming $A$, conclude $A \\to B$; from $A \\to B$ and $A$, conclude $B$. Read $\\to$ as \"implies\", and these are the rules for implication in logic. The next lesson asks whether that is a coincidence.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
